body:
I'm curious if this can be revisited with this new approach:

``` glsl
luminance = max(source_color.r, max(source_color.g, source_color.b));

// This formula should be more "accurate" but gave an overexposed result when testing.
// Leaving it here so we can revisit it if we want.
// luminance = source_color.r * 0.21 + source_color.g * 0.71 + source_color.b * 0.07;
```

It seems quite clear from the [Wikipedia page on photographic exposure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exposure_(photography)) that exposure (and auto-exposure) should be based off of luminance, not based off of `max(source_color.r, max(source_color.g, source_color.b))`. Maybe with better tonemappers like the AgX tonemapper combined with this PR it is no longer necessary to have the hacky non-luminance based calculation...??

If you do end up revisiting this, the luminance can be adjusted as follows to give clarity and consistency with the rest of the Godot code:

``` glsl
const vec3 rec709_luminance_weights = vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722);
luminance = dot(source_color.rgb, rec709_luminance_weights);
```
